we begin with breaking news the irish  government and apple  have won their appeal against a 2016 eu  decision  that said apple had to pay the irish  government 13 billion euros  in back taxes money that ireland didn't  even want  so ireland took the case to europe's  second highest court  dublin has long denied that its tax  arrangements with apple amount to  illegal state aid  the european commission is expected to  appeal the ruling  to the european court of justice  early this year then irish prime  minister leo varadkar and apple chief  executive tim cook  had an anniversary to celebrate 40 years  since apple started manufacturing in  ireland  over the decades the eu country and the  us iphone maker have both profited from  the arrangement  apple gets a massive tax break but it  provides employment  from multinational companies the  favorable situation in ireland is  practically unrivaled in europe  at the moment firms officially pay 12.5  corporate tax in ireland that's compared  with the eu average of 21.3 percent  officially companies in germany have to  cough up at least 30 percent  that alone has attracted many companies  to ireland  and in fact apple paid far less tax at  times  for example as little as 0.005  in 2014 when the tech giant paid just 50  euros tax on every million euros in  profit  that was the last straw for eu  competition commissioner marguerite  vestaia  in 2016 the european commission ordered  apple to pay  ireland 13 billion euros in back taxes  but dublin and apple both appealed the  ruling  well i'm now joined by arthur sullivan  from dw business who's looked  a little bit closer to the story it  looks like a major  uh vindication for countries the low tax  uh policies  uh like ireland so where does this leave  the european commission in their fight  against tax avoidance  well it's definitely a blow guard  symbolically it's a big blow but it is  important to draw a distinction between  this specific case and the wider fight  against tax avoidance so this case  looked at the issue of illegal state aid  did apple's specific arrangements in  ireland amount to illegal state aid  the courts today said it didn't that the  commission did not prove that it did  on on various levels and the the verdict  is quite clear in that regard  however the wider issue that is that  countries like ireland and the  netherlands and so on so forth  that the commission increasingly has  been looking the the competition  commissioner  margaret vestar has been increasingly  looking to tackle this issue  and we're here seeing more and more  reports that the  uh the commission are increasingly  willing to use tools they've never used  before  such as bringing in voting on changing  the corporate tax structures in the eu  not by totally uh all 27 members  agreeing but by a qualified majority now  if that comes in  that would mean that the fight is still  very much on but how  does this ruling how will this go down  uh  across capitals indeed depends on what  capitals you're talking about in places  like dublin the hague i suspect that  it'll be going down very well  places like paris and berlin much less  so and the big  backdrop of all this of course is that  this week european leaders are meeting  to discuss the pandemic recovery fund  so they're asking the leaders of  countries and governments to give  unprecedented sums of money that will be  given as grants  that they've never done before while at  the same time they're seeing a court  ruling today that grants companies like  apple  a fortune and the figures that we saw  like where they paid  0.000 tax so  obviously that is a very very difficult  backdrop to argue on one level you're  telling  the leaders of certain countries we need  your money while apple is  merely taking its money to who knows  where so  obviously it's a very very difficult  context so certainly there will be a  challenging reaction and i have no doubt  it will come up at the talks that are  happening this week  so is this the end of the affair no  certainly not the european commission  although it hasn't confirmed this yet  but it would be a shock if they weren't  going to appeal this to the highest  court  the european court of justice and if  that happens we'll be waiting for  another two years we could be back here  in two years time talking about this  gerhard  and the overall fight against tax  avoidance in in europe and and the world  as a whole  one that the oecd is involved in that's  not going anywhere the waters are rising  on this issue it's a blow to the  commission in their fight a significant  blow but this is not over  arthur sullivan thank you very much  you
 

